{"p":"/performance-optimization","t":"Performance optimization","d":"Introduces the performance optimization process for games on Roblox.","h":"<p>Performance optimization is the process of building and iterating on a game to improve frame rate, memory usage, and load times. Players expect games to perform well, so taking the time to understand and improve your game's performance can be critical to its success.</p>\n<p>The first step in performance optimization is to <a href=\"#/docs/performance-optimization/design\">design for performance</a>, which means following a set of best practices <strong>as you build</strong> a new game. After you publish a game, the optimization process follows a common cycle:</p>\n<div class=\"gridc\">\n<figure>\n1. [Identify performance Issues](#/docs/performance-optimization/identify) - Use Roblox's built-in tools to find performance problems and identify root causes.\n1. [Improve performance](#/docs/performance-optimization/improve) - After you find an issue, see if it's a common one and how you might mitigate it. You might also want to take proactive action.\n1. [Monitor performance](#/docs/performance-optimization/monitor) - Use analytics tools to monitor the impact of your changes and watch for indicators of any performance issues.\n</figure>\n<figure>\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"A diagram showing the flow from designing, identifying, improving, and monitoring performance.\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" />\n</figure>\n</div>\n<h2>Why performance matters</h2>\n<p>To put it simply, performant games are more fun. When a game has a consistent, smooth frame rate, even basic actions like running and jumping feel more precise and enjoyable. Aiming can go from feeling impossible to feeling completely natural. A game with a small memory footprint and fast load times might always have plenty of other people to play with.</p>\n<p>Performance optimization also lets you expand your ambitions. If your game runs well, you might be able to make it more expansive or beautiful or add new systems to it.</p>\n<p>From a business perspective, performant games often have higher user engagement, better user retention, and make more money.</p>\n<h2>Measure performance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frame rate</strong> is the number of unique frames that the client presents to the user, measured in frames per second (FPS). The cause of low frame rates is typically expensive computational operations—in other words, overtaxing the CPU, GPU, or both.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Because the server doesn't render frames, it doesn't <em>technically</em> have a frame rate, but server heartbeat is essentially the same thing: the number of times per second that the server updates its simulation of the world. A low server heartbeat reduces the frame rate for all clients and increases latency.</p>\n<p>On the client, low or inconsistent frame rates reduce the smoothness and responsiveness of the game. By default, Roblox games target 60 FPS, which is one frame every 16.67 milliseconds with proper frame pacing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Memory usage</strong> is the amount of RAM or swap that your game uses. Even if a game has low starting memory usage, memory leaks can cause that amount to increase over time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On the server, excessive memory usage can cause crashes, which disconnect all players from the game.</p>\n<p>Excessive memory usage causes client crashes, too, but it also prevents users on lower-end devices from playing your game in the first place. Reducing memory usage can greatly expand your addressable audience, especially on mobile.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Join time</strong> is the total time from when a player clicks the play button to when the world is rendered and they can move.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Long join times hurt user acquisition and retention—players may leave before ever entering your experience.</p>","kid":{"simple":"Performance optimization means making your game run smoothly, use less memory, and load faster. Smooth games feel better to play, work on more devices, and keep players around longer. You start by building smart from day one, then repeat a cycle: find problems, fix them, and keep watching. This page explains the three big things to measure: frame rate, memory, and join time.","analogy":"It is like taking care of a bike. You build it well, then you keep checking for squeaks, fix them, and ride it to make sure the fix worked.","keyPoints":["First, design for performance while you build. It is easier than fixing everything later.","After publishing, repeat the cycle: identify issues, improve performance, then monitor the results.","Smooth, fast games are more fun, keep players longer, and usually earn more.","Frame rate is how many pictures per second the player sees. Roblox games aim for 60 FPS, which is one frame every 16.67 milliseconds.","The server has a heartbeat instead of a frame rate. A low server heartbeat makes the game laggy for everyone.","Memory usage is how much RAM your game takes. Too much can crash the server (kicking everyone) or crash players' devices.","Join time is how long it takes from pressing Play to being able to move. Long join times make players leave before they start."],"terms":{"performance":"How smoothly and quickly your game runs.","frame rate (FPS)":"Frames per second, how many new pictures the screen shows each second. 60 FPS means 60 pictures every second.","millisecond (ms)":"One thousandth of a second. At 60 FPS, each frame has about 16.67 ms: 1000 / 60 = 16.67.","frame pacing":"Showing frames at even, steady times so movement looks smooth, not jumpy.","CPU":"The main brain of a device. It runs your scripts and physics.","GPU":"The graphics chip. It draws what you see on screen.","server heartbeat":"How many times per second the server updates the game world. It is like the server's version of frame rate.","latency":"Delay between doing something and the game reacting, like the time between pressing jump and seeing it happen online.","RAM":"Short-term memory on a device, where the running game keeps its stuff.","swap":"Backup memory on storage that a device uses when RAM is full. It is much slower.","memory leak":"When a game keeps holding on to memory it no longer needs, so usage keeps growing over time.","join time":"Time from clicking Play until the world shows and you can move.","retention":"How many players come back to play again."},"watchOut":["Waiting until the end to think about performance. Building with good habits from the start saves a lot of work.","Only testing on a powerful computer. Many players use phones with much less memory.","Ignoring the server. A slow or crashing server hurts every player in it, not just one."]}}